^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ literature review ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```ADD_PAPER
2502.06768v3
This paper investigates masked diffusion models (MDMs) with a focus on the impact of token ordering during training and inference. It provides both theoretical and empirical evidence that MDMs are challenged by training on a vast number of masking subproblems—many of which are computationally intractable when the natural token order (e.g., left-to-right for text) is not respected. The paper then introduces adaptive inference strategies, such as "top probability" and "top probability margin," to dynamically choose the token unmasking order during inference. These adaptive strategies allow the model to circumvent hard subproblems encountered during vanilla MDM inference, leading to dramatic improvements on challenging tasks. For example, the adaptive scheme boosts Sudoku puzzle solving accuracy from less than 7% to almost 90%, and similar gains are reported on coding, math, and natural language tasks. Extensive experimental analyses include comparisons of scaling laws on text datasets, likelihood modeling measurements that highlight performance imbalances, and evaluations on logic puzzles, where adaptive MDM inference even outperforms autoregressive models trained with ordering information. This work is highly relevant for designing adaptive token-ordering strategies in MDMs to maximize both accuracy and likelihood, offering insights into overcoming the inherent training complexities of order-agnostic models.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
