Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Adaptive Token-Ordering for Masked Diffusion Models: A Comparative Study with Autoregressive Models

Overview:
We propose a simple yet innovative experimental design to analyze and expand adaptive token-ordering strategies in Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) versus Autoregressive Models (ARMs) across text generation and logic puzzle benchmarks. The design is inspired by recent works on adaptive MDM inference (e.g., “P2”, ReCOR, and Any-Order GPT) and will reproduce baseline phenomena while integrating new ablations and oracle variants.

Experiment Outline:

1. Data & Model Setup
   a. Datasets:
      • Text: Curate a subset of standard language modeling benchmarks (e.g., WikiText, PG-19) for likelihood and scaling-law analysis.
      • Logic puzzles: Use synthetic logic puzzles like L&O-NAE-SAT, Sudoku, and the Zebra puzzle.
   b. Models:
      • MDMs:
         – For text: Train two scales – a 170M and 1.1B parameter MDM using an absorbing-mask forward process (no explicit time embedding) and π-learners to capture count-based noise levels.
         – For puzzles: Train a small MDM (6–19M parameters).
      • ARMs:
         – Train a 42M parameter ARM for puzzles, in two variants: one with teacher-forced (order-aware) supervision and one unsupervised.
         – Optionally, use LLaMA2-7B as an external evaluator for generative perplexity.
      
2. Training Loss and Subproblem Hardness Assessment
   • For each model, compute text validation loss, generative perplexity, and subproblem-level error metrics.
   • Compare training-time hardness of order-agnostic MDMs vs. order-aware ARMs. Log per-token predictive uncertainty and track the evolution of token demasking “hardness” across iterations.

3. Inference Strategies: Adaptive Token-Unmasking
   a. Implement adaptive inference oracles:
      • Top-K probability: At each iteration, unmask the K tokens with highest prediction probability.
      • Top-K probability margin: Unmask tokens based on the margin between the top two probabilities.
      • Stochastic variant: Inject temperature or Gumbel noise in the oracle to encourage diverse unmasking orders.
   b. Ablation study:
      • Vary K (batch size of tokens updated per step).
      • Test different noise settings (temperature parameters).
      • Evaluate the effect of different positional encoding schemes (learned vs. RoPE).
      • Examine how closely the inferred unmasking order matches the identity permutation.
      
4. Experiment Families
   Family 1: Text Likelihood & Scaling Law Analysis
      • Compare π-learner based MDMs with ARMs on text datasets.
      • Metrics: validation loss, perplexity, and token-level prediction entropy.
      • Analyze scaling behaviors over model sizes.
      
   Family 2: Synthetic L&O-NAE-SAT Analysis
      • Design a synthetic benchmark to expose subproblem imbalance.
      • Track how many and which subproblems are deemed “hard” by adaptive inference.
      • Compare oracle performance with fixed/random token orders.
      
   Family 3: Logic Puzzle Accuracy (Sudoku, Zebra)
      • Use logic puzzles to demonstrate dramatic gains with adaptive inference (e.g., raise Sudoku accuracy from ~7% to near 90%).
      • Baseline: ARM variants with teacher-forced ordering.
      • Core metrics: puzzle solve accuracy, number of reverse steps, and per-step error logging.

5. Implementation Details & Reproducibility
   • All experiments run with fixed random seeds and repeatability across at least 3 seeds.
   • Log every sampling path and per-step logits for detailed post-hoc analysis.
   • Use standardized logging to capture subproblem error metrics, entropy, and convergence time.
   
Timeline & Innovation:
   • The experiment leverages adaptive inference strategies that bridge theoretical insights with practical performance gains in both discrete puzzles and natural language generation.
   • Innovative use of π-learners and different oracle variants (Top-K, margin, and stochastic variants) to robustly challenge fixed ordering assumptions.
   • Direct comparisons across problem domains (text vs. puzzles) will yield insights into modality-specific inference adaptations.
   
Final Deliverables:
   • A technical report benchmarking adaptive token-ordering MDMs versus ARMs.
   • Open-sourced code implementations for all oracle variants and ablations.
   • Detailed analysis of training-time token hardness and inference dynamics.

This plan offers a clear pathway to replicate influential findings while introducing novel ablations and cross-domain comparisons. Let’s move forward with this design.
                    
 
