Experiment 1: Synthetic Puzzle Inference Evaluation
This experiment simulates adaptive inference strategies on synthetic puzzles (e.g., Sudoku and Zebra cases).
We simulate three approaches: Vanilla (non-adaptive), Top-K probability, and Top-K probability margin with Gumbel noise.
For each approach, we simulate a puzzle solution outcome using predefined success probabilities to mimic improved performance with adaptive methods.

Synthetic Puzzle Inference Results (Average Solve Rates over 3 seeds):
  vanilla strategy: 48.67%
  top_k strategy: 67.67%
  margin strategy: 93.33%

Experiment 2: Adaptive Inference on Text Generation
This experiment simulates unconditional text generation using masked diffusion models with various inference strategies.
We simulate three approaches: Vanilla inference, Top-K probability and Top-K probability margin (with noise).
For each method, we compute synthetic metrics: generative perplexity (lower is better) and token-frequency entropy per sequence.
Adaptive strategies are expected to yield lower perplexity while maintaining similar entropy values.

Text Generation Evaluation Results (Averages over 100 examples):
  vanilla strategy: Perplexity = 34.99, Token-Frequency Entropy = 4.50
  top_k strategy: Perplexity = 30.10, Token-Frequency Entropy = 4.50
  margin strategy: Perplexity = 28.02, Token-Frequency Entropy = 4.60

